# Introduction

The tasks at hand explore two key statistical methodologies: Bayesian inference in the context of a Binomial distribution and Bayesian linear regression. Task 1 revisits the estimation of Covid-19 prevalence during the early stages of the pandemic in Austria. Leveraging Bayesian principles, the task constructs a prior distribution informed by German data, derives the posterior distribution for the proportion of infected individuals, and interprets the results. This approach highlights the advantages of Bayesian methods in situations where data are scarce or standard frequentist methods fail. Task 2 delves into Bayesian linear regression, focusing on the inference of model parameters under conjugate priors. Using the well-known Auto dataset, we compare the Bayesian results to their frequentist counterparts for a simple linear model relating fuel efficiency (mpg) to engine horsepower. Together, these tasks demonstrate the power and flexibility of Bayesian methods in addressing complex statistical problems, offering insight into why they are chosen over traditional approaches in specific contexts.

\pagebreak

# Task 1

In the early stages of the Covid-19 pandemic, estimating the prevalence of the disease posed significant challenges due to limited data and low infection rates. Task 1 focuses on recalculating the prevalence of Covid-19 in Austria during spring 2020 using a Bayesian approach. With no positive cases detected in a sample of 1279 individuals, traditional frequentist methods fail to provide meaningful estimates. To address this, we incorporate prior information from a similar German study, where 4 positive cases were identified out of 4068 tests. By constructing a Beta prior distribution informed by the German data, and updating it with the Austrian data through Bayesian inference, we derive a posterior distribution for the prevalence. This task explores the calculation of point estimates, the 95% Highest Posterior Density (HPD) interval, and the rationale behind using Bayesian inference over frequentist or simulation-based methods in such scenarios.

### 1.1 Build a Beta prior distribution for this Binomial scenario, which encodes the information of the German study. Reweight both parameters compared to the original counts with a factor of 1/10.

This task focuses on constructing a Bayesian prior distribution for estimating Covid-19 prevalence in Austria during spring 2020. Using data from a German study with 4 positive cases out of 4068 tests, a Beta prior is built to encode this information. To reflect the relative importance of this prior, both parameters of the Beta distribution are scaled by a factor of 110. This prior will serve as the foundation for further Bayesian analysis of the Austrian data.

``` {r}
german_positive <- 4
german_total <- 4068

alpha_prior <- german_positive * (1/10)
beta_prior <- (german_total - german_positive) * (1/10)

alpha_prior
beta_prior
```
The code constructs a Beta prior distribution to model the Covid-19 prevalence in Austria based on a German study with 4 positive cases out of 4068 tests. The prior is adjusted by scaling both parameters α and β by a factor of 1/10. The resulting parameters are α=0.4 and β=406.4, which encode the information of 4 positives and 4064 negatives with reduced weight. This Beta prior will serve as the starting point for Bayesian analysis of the Austrian data.

