### 1.2 Build the corresponding Binomial model for the number of people suffering from the disease based on the 1279 test. Obtain the theoretical posterior distribution for this scenario.

In this part of the task, we extend the Bayesian analysis by building a Binomial model to estimate the number of people suffering from Covid-19 in Austria based on a sample of 1279 individuals, where no positive cases were detected. Using the Beta prior distribution established from the German study, we apply Bayes' theorem to derive the posterior distribution for the proportion of infected individuals in the Austrian population. This allows us to update our beliefs about the prevalence of the disease, taking into account both the prior knowledge from Germany and the observed data from Austria.

``` {r}
austrian_positive <- 0
austrian_total <- 1279

alpha_posterior <- alpha_prior + austrian_positive
beta_posterior <- beta_prior + (austrian_total - austrian_positive)

alpha_posterior
beta_posterior
```

The code updates the Beta prior distribution using the observed data from Austria, where no positive cases were detected out of 1279 tests. The prior parameters α=0.4 and β=406.4 are updated to αposterior=0.4 and βposterior=1685.4, reflecting the new information. This updated Beta distribution represents the posterior belief about the Covid-19 prevalence in Austria, combining the prior knowledge from Germany with the Austrian data, which suggests that the proportion of infected individuals is still likely to be very low, with most of the probability mass concentrated on low infection rates.

