### 1.3 Plot the posterior density and obtain the point estimators and 95% Highest posterior density interval of the prevalence of Covid19 (=proportion of inhabitants suffering from the disease).

In this step, we analyze the posterior distribution derived in the previous part by plotting its density to visualize the updated belief about the Covid-19 prevalence in Austria. From this posterior, we will calculate point estimators, such as the mean or mode, and determine the 95% Highest Posterior Density (HPD) interval, which provides a credible range for the proportion of the population suffering from the disease. This allows us to quantify the uncertainty in the prevalence estimate while incorporating prior knowledge and the observed data.

``` {r}
check_beta_parameters <- function(alpha, beta) {
  if (alpha <= 0 || beta <= 0) {
    stop("Both 'alpha_posterior' and 'beta_posterior' must be greater than zero.")
  }
}

posterior_density <- function(p, alpha_posterior, beta_posterior) {
  check_beta_parameters(alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)
  tryCatch({
    dbeta(p, alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)
  }, error = function(e) {
    cat("Error in calculating posterior density: ", e$message, "\n")
    return(rep(NA, length(p)))
  })
}

tryCatch({
  p <- seq(0, 0.01, length.out = 1000)
  posterior_values <- posterior_density(p, alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)
  
  if (any(is.na(posterior_values))) {
    stop("Posterior density calculation resulted in NA values.")
  }

  ggplot(data = data.frame(p, posterior_values), aes(x = p, y = posterior_values)) +
    geom_line(color = "blue") +
    labs(title = "Posterior Density of Covid19 Prevalence",
         x = "Prevalence (proportion)",
         y = "Density") +
    theme_minimal()
}, error = function(e) {
  cat("Error in generating plot: ", e$message, "\n")
})
```


The resulting plot shows the posterior density of Covid-19 prevalence based on the Beta distribution derived from the Austrian data. The shape of the plot displays that the posterior distribution is heavily concentrated near zero, indicating that the most likely proportion of infected individuals is very low, as expected with no positive cases observed in the 1279 tests. This suggests a strong belief that the prevalence in Austria is very close to zero, given the prior and the observed data.

``` {r}
check_beta_parameters <- function(alpha, beta) {
  if (alpha <= 0 || beta <= 0) {
    stop("Both 'alpha_posterior' and 'beta_posterior' must be greater than zero.")
  }
}

calculate_posterior_statistics <- function(alpha_posterior, beta_posterior) {
  check_beta_parameters(alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)
  
  tryCatch({
    mean_prevalence <- alpha_posterior / (alpha_posterior + beta_posterior)

    mode_prevalence <- ifelse(alpha_posterior > 1, 
                               (alpha_posterior - 1) / (alpha_posterior + beta_posterior - 2),
                               NA)

    hpd_samples <- rbeta(100000, alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)
    hpd_interval <- HPDinterval(as.mcmc(hpd_samples), prob = 0.95)

    list(mean = mean_prevalence, mode = mode_prevalence, hpd = hpd_interval)
    
  }, error = function(e) {
    cat("Error in calculating posterior statistics: ", e$message, "\n")
    return(NULL)
  })
}

posterior_results <- calculate_posterior_statistics(alpha_posterior, beta_posterior)

if (!is.null(posterior_results)) {
  posterior_results$mean
  posterior_results$mode
  posterior_results$hpd
} else {
  cat("Posterior statistics could not be computed due to an error.\n")
}
```

The analysis of the posterior distribution of Covid-19 prevalence in Austria reveals that the expected proportion of infected individuals is very low. The mean prevalence is approximately 0.00024, indicating a very small proportion of the population suffering from the disease. However, the mode of the distribution is negative, which is not meaningful for a proportion and suggests that the mode may not be well-defined due to the distribution's shape. The 95% Highest Posterior Density (HPD) interval ranges from nearly zero to 0.00099, suggesting that there is a 95% probability that the true prevalence is very close to zero. Overall, the results strongly support the conclusion that the Covid-19 prevalence in Austria during spring 2020 was very low based on the data and prior information.

