### 1.4 Explain why Statistik Austria chose this method instead of simulationbased or frequentist inference for obtaining intervals of the prevalence.

Statistik Austria likely chose a Bayesian approach for several compelling reasons. First, it allows the integration of prior knowledge, such as data from Germany, to improve the accuracy of prevalence estimates when local data is limited or scarce. Second, Bayesian methods effectively handle cases with zero positive results, a scenario where frequentist approaches often fail by yielding undefined or unreliable interval estimates. Third, Bayesian credible intervals, such as the highest posterior density intervals, provide a more intuitive interpretation by identifying the range of values with the highest probability given the data. Finally, Bayesian inference proves robust in situations involving small sample sizes or rare events, which was particularly critical during the early stages of the pandemic when information was scarce.

