# Task 2

Task 2 delves into Bayesian inference within the context of linear regression, specifically focusing on the estimation of the model's coefficient (β) and the residual variance (σ2). In Bayesian linear modeling, we assume that the residuals follow a normal distribution, and we use conjugate priors for both parameters to facilitate analytical tractability. The task involves defining these priors, exploring how to set them to be uninformative, and comparing the effects of different prior choices. By building the normal regression model, we derive the posterior distributions for β and σ2, and calculate the corresponding point estimators and 95% Highest Posterior Density (HPD) intervals. We then apply this methodology to the Auto dataset from Exercise 6, comparing the results with those obtained from frequentist methods. This task highlights the advantages of Bayesian inference in incorporating prior knowledge and updating beliefs based on observed data.

### 2.1 Define conjugate priors for the coefficient parameter and the residual variance independently. Explain how the parameters can be set to be uninformative. Compare different choice of prior parameters.

In this step, we define conjugate priors for the parameters of a Bayesian linear regression model: the regression coefficient (β) and the residual variance (σ2). Conjugate priors are chosen because they simplify the calculation of the posterior distribution by ensuring that the prior and likelihood belong to the same family of distributions. For β, a normal prior is typically used, while for σ2, an inverse Gamma prior is common. We also explore how these priors can be set to be uninformative, meaning they reflect minimal prior knowledge and allow the data to predominantly influence the posterior. Finally, we compare the effects of different prior choices on the regression results, providing insight into how prior specifications can impact Bayesian inference.

